# Evidence: the UI contracts and the prototype screens compile inside the REAL v4.1.4 bootstrap (2026-11-05)
#
# 1. An empty repository was bootstrapped exactly as preflight-tooling.yml does it:
#      git init app414
#      dotnet requirements-compiler.dll v41 --source . --package requirements-compiled/v4.1.4 --base requirements-compiled/v4 --bootstrap app414
#      -> "v4.1.4 validation: PASS; status: CANDIDATE"
#    The bootstrap writes the frozen IF41 types into src/Causa.App/Engine.fs (ConnectionState, Msg, Model, Engine) and
#    src/Causa.App/Projections.fs (SaveAction, SaveIndicatorView, ...). Every view file (Views/Investigation.fs, ...) is an
#    empty "the owner replaces this content" stub. Causa.Domain holds the frozen stubs only: no kernel exists.
# 2. contracts/{Ids,Types,UiStates,Navigation,Connection,InvestigationList,InvestigationDetail,InvestigationEditor,
#    InvestigationAnalysis,System,UiActions,Bindings}.fs and prototype/screens/{Shell,ConnectionScreen,
#    InvestigationListScreen,Events}.fs were copied unchanged into src/Causa.App/Ui/, and listed in Causa.App.fsproj
#    directly after Projections.fs. (The frozen files are NOT linked here: the bootstrap's own files define them.)
# 3. dotnet build Causa.slnx -v q
       0 Warning(s)
   Build succeeded.
   real 0m24.363s
#
# Meaning: the contract files are source-compatible with the real project at T10, so a UI agent's screen module compiles
# in the real application from bootstrap onward, with no domain, persistence or GitHub implementation. It does not mean
# the screens work against real data (Layer C).
